abstract: >-
  Brazil's scheduled domestic flights of 2000-2013, from the Voo Regular Ativo
  (VRA) flight-leg records that ANAC publishes -- 13,652,322 legs in 168
  monthly files -- to the questions Bendinelli, Bettini & Oliveira (2016,
  Transportation Research Part A 85, 39-52, doi:10.1016/j.tra.2016.01.001)
  asked of them. Four products: the article's own estimation panel (24,589
  route-months x 52 columns, 2002-2013), the open reconstruction of the VRA
  into a fact table, a route-month panel of 31,313 route-months x 228 columns
  and two city projections, the congestion game the article draws on --
  Brueckner and Van Dender (2008) as set out in the author's 2013 monograph --
  derived symbolically with its 29 identities checked, and a flight-level delay
  predictor. Tables 2-7 re-estimated on the estimation panel track the
  published tables: of 306 coefficients, 302 carry the published sign, 259 sit
  within half a published standard error and the largest gap is 0.94 standard
  errors; the re-estimated samples are 5.3 per cent larger than the published
  ones. The predictor is evaluated out
  of time over eight rolling-origin folds (2006-2013) at two horizons,
  day-ahead and at the gate, with leakage checks in the test suite. Code under
  MIT; text and curated data under CC BY 4.0; tables derived from the VRA
  attributed to ANAC, Voo Regular Ativo (VRA), via dados.gov.br.
